-- Q1 [Excitation-Contraction Coupling] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'An action potential travels down the axon of a somatic motor neuron and reaches the axon terminal at the neuromuscular junction of a skeletal muscle fiber. Which chemical messenger is most directly released into the synaptic cleft to bind receptors on the motor end-plate?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Acetylcholine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Acetylcholinesterase"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Calcium ions"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Norepinephrine"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the axon terminal of a somatic motor neuron releases acetylcholine into the synaptic cleft, where it binds receptors on the motor end-plate to begin excitation. The textbook states, "At the NMJ, the axon terminal releases a chemical messenger, or neurotransmitter, called acetylcholine (ACh). The ACh molecules diffuse across a minute space called the synaptic cleft and bind to ACh receptors located within the motor end-plate of the sarcolemma" (lines 17276-17278). The source also lists, for the NMJ, that "the neurotransmitter is acetylcholine" (line 22219). Binding opens a receptor channel that lets positively charged ions enter and depolarize the fiber, initiating excitation-contraction coupling. (Choice A) Acetylcholine is correct: it is the neurotransmitter packaged in vesicles in the presynaptic terminal and exocytosed into the cleft to act on the end-plate. (Choice B) Acetylcholinesterase is the enzyme that degrades ACh in the cleft so it "cannot rebind to a receptor and reopen its channel" (lines 17289-17291); it terminates the signal rather than being the released messenger. (Choice C) Calcium ions are the intracellular trigger inside the terminal — when the action potential arrives, "voltage-gated Ca2+ channels in the membrane of the synaptic end bulb open" and \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) "facilitates the merging of the vesicle with the presynaptic membrane" (lines 22225-22228), so \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) causes ACh release but is not itself the messenger crossing the cleft to the end-plate. (Choice D) Norepinephrine is a biogenic-amine transmitter of adrenergic synapses released into the blood and at autonomic sites (lines 22283-22290), not the transmitter at the somatic skeletal NMJ. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the somatic motor neuron releases acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction to depolarize the motor end-plate.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
