-- Q2 [Excitation-Contraction Coupling] easy skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'A motor neuron releases acetylcholine into the synaptic cleft, and the \(\text{ACh}\) binds receptors in the motor end-plate of a skeletal muscle fiber. What happens at the end-plate most directly as a result of this binding?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum into the cytosol"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{Na}^+\\) enters the fiber and depolarizes the sarcolemma"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{K}^+\\) exits the fiber and repolarizes the sarcolemma"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Acetylcholinesterase degrades \\(\\text{ACh}\\) in the synaptic cleft"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because \(\text{ACh}\) binding opens a cation channel in the end-plate receptor, letting positively charged \(\text{Na}^+\) flow into the fiber and depolarize the sarcolemma. The textbook states that "Once ACh binds, a channel in the ACh receptor opens and positively charged ions can pass through into the muscle fiber, causing it to depolarize, meaning that the membrane potential of the muscle fiber becomes less negative." Because this receptor is a cation channel, "positively charged Na+ will rush into the cell," driving the membrane toward threshold so that voltage-gated sodium channels then open and an action potential fires. This local end-plate depolarization is the initiating electrical event of excitation-contraction coupling. (Choice A) \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum is a real EC-coupling step, but it occurs later: the action potential propagating along the sarcolemma "triggers the release of calcium ions (Ca++) from its storage in the cell''s SR," so SR calcium release is downstream of end-plate depolarization, not the direct result of \(\text{ACh}\) binding. (Choice B) is correct: \(\text{ACh}\) opens a cation channel and inward \(\text{Na}^+\) depolarizes the end-plate, the first step toward the muscle action potential. (Choice C) reverses both the ion movement and the outcome; \(\text{K}^+\) efflux producing repolarization is the opposite event, occurring "immediately following depolarization of the membrane," when "it repolarizes, re-establishing the negative membrane potential." (Choice D) acetylcholinesterase does act at this synapse, but it degrades \(\text{ACh}\) "so that the ACh cannot rebind to a receptor," terminating signaling rather than producing the depolarization. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that \(\text{ACh}\) binding opens a cation channel allowing \(\text{Na}^+\) influx that depolarizes the sarcolemma.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
