-- Q3 [Excitation-Contraction Coupling] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'A researcher uses a detergent to selectively detach the T-tubules from the rest of the sarcolemma in an isolated skeletal muscle fiber, leaving the surface membrane and sarcoplasmic reticulum otherwise intact. When the surface sarcolemma is then stimulated to fire an action potential, the deep myofibrils in the fiber''s interior most likely fail to contract because the action potential is no longer carried to the interior to trigger which event?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Acetylcholine release into the synaptic cleft"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Reuptake of \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) into the sarcoplasmic reticulum"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Opening of \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) channels in the sarcoplasmic reticulum"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Binding of \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) to troponin on the thin filament"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because the T-tubules carry the action potential into the cell''s interior, where it triggers the opening of calcium channels in the membrane of the adjacent sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). The textbook states, "The T-tubules carry the action potential into the interior of the cell, which triggers the opening of calcium channels in the membrane of the adjacent SR, causing Ca++ to diffuse out of the SR and into the sarcoplasm" (lines 17308–17310). T-tubules are "periodic invaginations in the sarcolemma" that exist precisely so "the action potential [can] reach the membrane of the SR," ensuring "the membrane can get close to the SR in the sarcoplasm" deep inside a fiber up to 100 μm wide (lines 17294–17297). If the T-tubules are detached, the depolarization cannot propagate to the SR in the interior, so the SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels never open and the deep myofibrils receive no \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) signal. (Choice A) Acetylcholine release occurs upstream at the neuromuscular junction from the motor neuron''s axon terminal, before any action potential travels along the sarcolemma, so it is not the event the T-tubules deliver the impulse to trigger (lines 17275–17285). (Choice B) Reuptake of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) into the SR is part of relaxation—"Ca++ ions are then pumped back into the SR"—which happens when signaling stops, the reverse of the release event a T-tubule action potential triggers (lines 17343–17345). (Choice C) is correct because the arrival of the action potential at the SR via the T-tubules is the direct trigger for SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channel opening and \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) efflux into the sarcoplasm. (Choice D) \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binding to troponin is a downstream consequence that can only occur after \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) has already been released from the SR into the sarcoplasm, so it is not the event the T-tubule directly triggers (lines 17332–17334). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that T-tubules conduct the action potential into the fiber interior to trigger SR calcium-channel opening.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
