-- Q4 [Excitation-Contraction Coupling] easy skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'During excitation-contraction coupling in a skeletal muscle fiber, \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum into the sarcoplasm. To which thin-filament protein does this released \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) bind directly to begin contraction?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Actin"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Myosin"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tropomyosin"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Troponin"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because troponin is the thin-filament regulatory protein that carries the binding site for \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\), so released \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binds troponin directly. The textbook states that troponin "also has a binding site for Ca++ ions" and that "the first step in the process of contraction is for Ca++ to bind to troponin so that tropomyosin can slide away from the binding sites on the actin strands." Once \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binds troponin, the troponin-tropomyosin complex shifts, exposing the myosin-binding sites on actin and allowing cross-bridges to form. Tropomyosin and actin are moved or unblocked as a downstream consequence, but neither is the molecule that \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) contacts. (Choice A) Actin bears the myosin-binding sites that become exposed, but it is the protein being uncovered, not the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) receptor; \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) does not bind actin to trigger this step. (Choice B) Myosin forms the thick-filament heads that bind exposed actin sites and hydrolyze \(\text{ATP}\); it is not part of the thin-filament regulatory complex and does not receive the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) signal. (Choice C) Tropomyosin blocks the myosin-binding sites at rest and slides away once contraction begins, but it moves only because \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binds troponin first; tropomyosin itself lacks the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binding site. (Choice D) Troponin is correct because it is the only thin-filament protein with a dedicated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binding subunit, making it the direct receptor whose conformational change repositions tropomyosin and initiates contraction. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that troponin, not tropomyosin or actin, carries the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binding site that triggers the thin-filament conformational change.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
