-- Q5 [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'During sustained skeletal muscle contraction, a single myosin head repeatedly attaches to actin, executes a power stroke, detaches, and re-cocks. Which molecule does the myosin head directly hydrolyze to power each round of this cross-bridge cycle?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{ATP}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Creatine phosphate"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{ADP}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{GTP}\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the intrinsic ATPase activity of the myosin head hydrolyzes \(\text{ATP}\) to \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\), and the energy released re-cocks the head for the next cross-bridge cycle. The textbook states that after \(\text{ATP}\) binding causes the myosin head to detach from actin, "\(\text{ATP}\) is converted to ADP and Pi by the intrinsic ATPase activity of myosin" and "The energy released during ATP hydrolysis changes the angle of the myosin head into a cocked position" (openstax-anatomy-physiology-2e.txt, lines 17439-17441). It further notes that the repetitive pulling of the myosin heads "requires energy, which is provided by ATP" (lines 17409-17411) and that "As long as ATP is available, it readily attaches to myosin, the cross-bridge cycle can recur, and muscle contraction can continue" (lines 17446-17447). (Choice A) \(\text{ATP}\) is correct: it is the molecule that binds the myosin head, is split by myosin''s own ATPase enzyme, and supplies the energy that drives every attach-pull-detach-recock cycle. (Choice B) Creatine phosphate is wrong because it is only an energy reserve that "transfers its phosphate back to ADP to form ATP" via creatine kinase (lines 17467-17469); it regenerates \(\text{ATP}\) but is never the molecule hydrolyzed by the myosin head itself. (Choice C) \(\text{ADP}\) is wrong because it is a product of \(\text{ATP}\) hydrolysis, not the fuel; it is released after the power stroke (line 17445), not split to release energy. (Choice D) \(\text{GTP}\) is wrong because, although it is a high-energy nucleotide used elsewhere (e.g., one \(\text{GTP}\)/\(\text{ATP}\) is generated per turn of the Krebs cycle, line 50509), myosin''s ATPase hydrolyzes \(\text{ATP}\), not \(\text{GTP}\), to power cross-bridge cycling. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that myosin''s intrinsic ATPase hydrolyzes \(\text{ATP}\) to power repeated cross-bridge cycling.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
