-- Q6 [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] easy skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'A motor neuron stops firing, but acetylcholine (ACh) molecules from the previous action potential still occupy receptors on the motor end-plate. Which event most directly allows the sarcolemma to repolarize and the muscle fiber to relax?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Voltage-gated \\(\\text{Na}^+\\) channels open at the end-plate"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) is pumped from the sarcoplasm back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tropomyosin reshields the binding sites on the actin filaments"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Acetylcholinesterase degrades the ACh in the synaptic cleft"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because the enzyme acetylcholinesterase (AChE) breaks down ACh in the synaptic cleft so the neurotransmitter cannot rebind its receptor and reopen the channel, ending stimulation and permitting the membrane to repolarize. The textbook states that the ACh in the synaptic cleft is degraded by the enzyme acetylcholinesterase (AChE) so that the ACh cannot rebind to a receptor and reopen its channel, which would cause unwanted extended muscle excitation and contraction. As long as ACh remains bound, its receptor channel stays open and positive ions keep entering, holding the membrane depolarized; clearing the ACh is therefore the most direct event that lets repolarization re-establish the negative membrane potential. (Choice A) Voltage-gated \(\text{Na}^+\) channels opening drives depolarization and propagation of the action potential, the opposite of repolarization, so this event would prolong rather than end stimulation. (Choice B) Pumping \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum is a real relaxation step, but it acts inside the fiber on the contractile machinery and follows repolarization; it does not clear ACh from the cleft or repolarize the sarcolemma. (Choice C) Tropomyosin reshielding actin-binding sites stops cross-bridge cycling and is a downstream consequence of falling \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\); it occurs on the thin filament and does not affect the ACh occupying end-plate receptors. (Choice D) Degradation of ACh by acetylcholinesterase removes the agonist from the cleft, closing the receptor channels so the sarcolemma can repolarize, which is the most direct trigger described. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that acetylcholinesterase degrades ACh in the synaptic cleft to terminate end-plate stimulation and permit repolarization.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
