-- Q7 [Excitation-Contraction Coupling] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'A physiologist labels the smallest functional grouping through which the nervous system commands a skeletal muscle to contract, noting that each fiber in the muscle receives input from only one motor neuron. Which grouping does this label most accurately identify?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A single muscle fiber and every motor neuron innervating it"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The progressive activation of additional motor units producing greater force"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A single motor neuron and all the muscle fibers it innervates"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The synapse where an axon terminal meets the sarcolemma"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because a motor unit is one motor neuron together with all of the muscle fibers that single neuron innervates. The textbook states, "The actual group of muscle fibers in a muscle innervated by a single motor neuron is called a motor unit," and notes that "Each muscle fiber is innervated by only one motor neuron" (anatomy-physiology, lines 17630-17632). A reinforcing passage confirms, "Together, the motor neuron and all the muscle fibers that it controls make up a motor unit" (lines 17814-17815), and the glossary defines it as the "motor neuron and the group of muscle fibers it innervates" (line 18315). Because one neuron may branch to many fibers but each fiber answers to a single neuron, the motor unit is the smallest all-or-none group the nervous system can command. (Choice A) reverses the innervation relationship: a single fiber is innervated by only one motor neuron, not by every neuron, so pairing one fiber with multiple neurons contradicts "Each muscle fiber is innervated by only one motor neuron" (line 17631). (Choice B) describes recruitment, defined as the "increase in the number of motor units involved in contraction" (line 18358); this is a force-grading process that uses motor units, not the definition of a motor unit itself. (Choice C) is correct because it names exactly one neuron plus the full set of fibers it controls, matching the textbook definition. (Choice D) describes the neuromuscular junction, the "specialized synaptic structure" where the axon terminal contacts the sarcolemma (lines 26821-26822); that is the connection point within a motor unit, not the neuron-plus-fibers grouping. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the textbook definition of a motor unit and the one-fiber-to-one-neuron innervation rule.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
