-- Q8 [Excitation-Contraction Coupling] medium skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'A motor neuron fires and a skeletal muscle fiber it innervates begins to contract. The following events are part of the early electrical signaling that leads to contraction:

\(\text{I.}\) Voltage-gated \(\text{Na}^+\) channels open and an action potential spreads along the sarcolemma.
\(\text{II.}\) The axon terminal releases acetylcholine (ACh) into the synaptic cleft.
\(\text{III.}\) The action potential is carried into the fiber''s interior by the T-tubules.
\(\text{IV.}\) ACh binds receptors in the motor end-plate and the membrane there depolarizes.

Which sequence most accurately orders these events from earliest to latest?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "II \\(\\rightarrow\\) IV \\(\\rightarrow\\) III \\(\\rightarrow\\) I"}, {"label": "B", "text": "II \\(\\rightarrow\\) IV \\(\\rightarrow\\) I \\(\\rightarrow\\) III"}, {"label": "C", "text": "IV \\(\\rightarrow\\) II \\(\\rightarrow\\) I \\(\\rightarrow\\) III"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II \\(\\rightarrow\\) I \\(\\rightarrow\\) IV \\(\\rightarrow\\) III"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because acetylcholine release (II) drives end-plate depolarization (IV), which opens voltage-gated \(\text{Na}^+\) channels to fire a sarcolemmal action potential (I), which is then carried inward by the T-tubules (III). The textbook states that signaling begins when the axon terminal releases ACh, which diffuses across the synaptic cleft and binds receptors in the motor end-plate so that positive ions enter and the membrane depolarizes; only then does it note that "as the membrane depolarizes, another set of ion channels called voltage-gated sodium channels are triggered to open. Sodium ions enter the muscle fiber, and an action potential rapidly spreads (or ''fires'') along the entire membrane." It then specifies that "the T-tubules carry the action potential into the interior of the cell," placing T-tubule conduction last among these four. The end-plate depolarization is a chemically gated (ligand-gated) event that must precede the voltage-gated event it triggers, fixing IV before I. (Choice A) This reverses the final two steps: the sarcolemmal action potential (I) must already exist before the T-tubules (III) can carry it inward, so III cannot precede I. (Choice B) This is correct because the ligand-gated end-plate depolarization (IV) opens the voltage-gated \(\text{Na}^+\) channels that propagate the action potential along the sarcolemma (I), which the T-tubules then conduct into the interior (III). (Choice C) This places end-plate depolarization (IV) before ACh release (II), but depolarization at the end-plate is caused by ACh binding its receptors, so the chemical messenger must be released first. (Choice D) This places the spreading sarcolemmal action potential (I) before the end-plate depolarizes (IV), but the voltage-gated \(\text{Na}^+\) channels that produce that action potential are only triggered "as the membrane depolarizes" at the end-plate, so IV must precede I. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the fixed sequence of early electrical events at the neuromuscular junction and along the sarcolemma during excitation-contraction coupling.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
