-- Q9 [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'During repetitive cross-bridge cycling in an actively contracting skeletal muscle fiber, an investigator tracks the nucleotide occupancy of a single myosin head and the timing of its mechanical events. Which of the following sequences correctly order three consecutive events as they occur within one cross-bridge cycle?

I. Inorganic phosphate (\(\text{P}_\text{i}\)) is released, then the power stroke pulls actin toward the M-line, then \(\text{ADP}\) is released.
II. A new \(\text{ATP}\) binds and detaches the head, then \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) is released, then the power stroke pulls actin toward the M-line.
III. The cocked head bearing \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) attaches to actin, then \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) is released, then the head pivots toward the M-line.', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I and II only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "II only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because sequences I and III each place \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release before the power stroke and the power stroke before \(\text{ADP}\) release, matching the textbook order, whereas II reverses the cycle by putting \(\text{ATP}\)-driven detachment before the power stroke. Cross-bridge formation occurs when the cocked myosin head attaches to actin while \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) are still bound; \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) is then released, causing a stronger attachment, after which the head moves toward the M-line in the power stroke, pulling actin about 10 nm (openstax-anatomy-physiology-2e.txt lines 17421-17424). After the power stroke, \(\text{ADP}\) is released while actin and myosin remain bound, and only then does a new \(\text{ATP}\) attach to cause detachment, followed by \(\text{ATP}\) hydrolysis that re-cocks the head (lines 17439-17446). (Choice A) Sequence I is correctly ordered, but II is not, because \(\text{ATP}\) binding/detachment does not precede \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release and a power stroke—detachment is followed by hydrolysis and re-cocking, and the head must rebind actin before any new power stroke. (Choice B) II alone is incorrect because it reverses the cycle: \(\text{ATP}\) binding causes detachment only after the power stroke and \(\text{ADP}\) release, so detachment cannot be immediately followed by \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release and a stroke. (Choice C) is correct because both I and III preserve the canonical order \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release → power stroke → \(\text{ADP}\) release, with III beginning one step earlier at attachment of the \(\text{ADP}\)/\(\text{P}_\text{i}\)-loaded cocked head. (Choice D) includes the reversed sequence II; although III is correctly ordered, II misplaces \(\text{ATP}\)-driven detachment before the power stroke, so the combination is wrong. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of the cross-bridge cycle to order specific molecular and mechanical events within a single situation.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
