-- Q10 [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'When a person dies and ATP production ceases, the myosin heads in skeletal muscle remain locked onto actin, producing the rigidity of rigor mortis. The molecular event that would normally release these myosin heads from actin is most directly triggered by which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Binding of a new \\(\\text{ATP}\\) molecule to the myosin head"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Release of inorganic phosphate \\(\\text{P}_\\text{i}\\) from the myosin head"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Release of \\(\\text{ADP}\\) from the myosin head"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Hydrolysis of \\(\\text{ATP}\\) to \\(\\text{ADP}\\) and \\(\\text{P}_\\text{i}\\) by myosin"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the binding of a new \(\text{ATP}\) molecule to the myosin head is the event that causes the cross-bridge to detach from actin. The textbook states that "ATP binding causes the myosin head to detach from the actin," and that "In the absence of ATP, the myosin head will not detach from actin." This is exactly why rigor mortis occurs: "With no further ATP production possible, there is no ATP available for myosin heads to detach from the actin-binding sites, so the cross-bridges stay in place, causing the rigidity in the skeletal muscles." Detachment requires \(\text{ATP}\) to occupy the myosin head''s separate ATP-binding site, which weakens its hold on actin. (Choice A) is correct: \(\text{ATP}\) binding directly weakens the actin–myosin link and releases the cross-bridge, so its absence after death keeps the heads bound. (Choice B) is wrong because release of \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) does the opposite of detachment — it causes myosin to form a stronger attachment to the actin and initiates the power stroke, not release. (Choice C) is wrong because after the power stroke "ADP is released; however, the formed cross-bridge is still in place, and actin and myosin are bound together," so \(\text{ADP}\) release leaves the head attached. (Choice D) is wrong because hydrolysis of \(\text{ATP}\) to \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) occurs only after detachment and re-cocks the head into the high-energy position ("The energy released during ATP hydrolysis changes the angle of the myosin head into a cocked position"); it does not itself separate the head from actin. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that ATP binding, not phosphate or ADP release, is the trigger that detaches the myosin cross-bridge from actin.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
