-- Q11 [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] medium skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'In a contracting skeletal muscle fiber, a myosin head has just attached to actin while \(\text{ADP}\) and inorganic phosphate \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) remain bound to it. The molecular event that most directly triggers the power stroke, sliding the thin filament toward the M-line, is the', '[{"label": "A", "text": "binding of a new \\(\\text{ATP}\\) molecule to the myosin head"}, {"label": "B", "text": "hydrolysis of \\(\\text{ATP}\\) into \\(\\text{ADP}\\) and \\(\\text{P}_\\text{i}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "binding of \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) to troponin"}, {"label": "D", "text": "release of \\(\\text{P}_\\text{i}\\) from the myosin head"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because release of inorganic phosphate from the actin-bound myosin head is the step that strengthens the attachment and drives the head to pivot toward the M-line. After the myosin head attaches to actin with \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) still bound, the textbook states that \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) is then released, causing myosin to form a stronger attachment to the actin, after which the myosin head moves toward the M-line, pulling the actin along with it (filaments move approximately 10 nm). This movement is named the power stroke because movement of the thin filament occurs at this step. The events that follow this step are \(\text{ATP}\) binding (detachment) and \(\text{ATP}\) hydrolysis (re-cocking), neither of which produces the power stroke. (Choice A) \(\text{ATP}\) binding causes the myosin head to detach from actin, ending the cross-bridge rather than driving the power stroke. (Choice B) Hydrolysis of \(\text{ATP}\) into \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) by myosin''s intrinsic ATPase re-cocks the head into the high-energy configuration; it precedes attachment and is the recovery step, not the power stroke. (Choice C) \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binding to troponin shifts tropomyosin to expose the actin-binding sites, an upstream regulatory step that permits cross-bridge formation but does not itself drive the sliding. (Choice D) Correct: with \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) initially bound, the release of \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) is the trigger that strengthens binding and powers the head''s swing toward the M-line, while \(\text{ADP}\) is released only afterward. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the ordered molecular events of the cross-bridge cycle and identify \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release as the trigger of the power stroke.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
