-- Q12 [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] medium skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'A skeletal muscle fiber stops receiving signals from its motor neuron, its sarcolemma repolarizes, and tension begins to fall. Which event most directly returns sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) to resting levels so that tropomyosin can re-cover the myosin-binding sites?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) diffuses down its gradient into the SR"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{ATP}\\)-driven pumps export \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) across the sarcolemma"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{ATP}\\)-driven pumps move \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) back into the SR"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Voltage-gated SR channels open to release \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because relaxation requires ATP-driven pumps to actively transport \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) out of the sarcoplasm and back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR), which lowers cytosolic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) and lets tropomyosin reshield the binding sites. Once the motor neuron stops signaling, the sarcolemma repolarizes and the SR calcium-release channels close, so \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) no longer enters the sarcoplasm; the textbook states that ''ATP-driven pumps will move Ca++ out of the sarcoplasm back into the SR,'' and that ''This results in the "reshielding" of the actin-binding sites on the thin filaments.'' Because the SR maintains \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) at a much higher concentration than the sarcoplasm, this transport runs against the gradient and therefore must be active, consuming ATP. With sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) lowered, \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) leaves troponin, tropomyosin moves back over the actin-binding sites, cross-bridge cycling stops, and the fiber loses tension. (Choice A) Diffusion into the SR is impossible here because the SR holds \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) far above sarcoplasmic levels, so moving \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) inward is against the gradient and requires ATP-driven active transport, not passive diffusion. (Choice B) Active export is the correct mechanism but the wrong destination: \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is pumped back into the SR, not out across the sarcolemma into the extracellular fluid. (Choice C) This is correct because the ATP-driven SR pump actively returns \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) to the SR, dropping sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) so tropomyosin re-covers the sites; the energy requirement is what links relaxation to ATP availability. (Choice D) Opening voltage-gated SR channels to release \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is the contraction-initiating step, the opposite of relaxation; during relaxation those channels close as the membrane repolarizes. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that muscle relaxation depends on an ATP-driven SR pump actively returning \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) to the SR.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
