-- Q13 [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] medium skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'A motor neuron stops firing, and a skeletal muscle fiber begins to relax. Consider three molecular events that occur during this shutdown:

I. \(\text{ATP}\)-driven pumps return \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) to the sarcoplasmic reticulum.
II. Tropomyosin re-covers the binding sites on the thin filaments.
III. The sarcolemma repolarizes and the SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release channels close.

Which sequence most accurately orders these events as the fiber relaxes?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "II only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "I, then II, then III"}, {"label": "C", "text": "II, then I, then III"}, {"label": "D", "text": "III, then I, then II"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because relaxation proceeds from repolarization and channel closure, to active \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) reuptake, to tropomyosin re-shielding of the binding sites. When the motor neuron stops releasing ACh, the muscle fiber repolarizes, which closes the SR gates that were releasing \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) (event III); \(\text{ATP}\)-driven pumps then move \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) out of the sarcoplasm back into the SR (event I); the drop in sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) causes tropomyosin to re-shield the actin-binding sites (event II), so cross-bridges can no longer form and the fiber loses tension. The textbook states this order directly: "The muscle fiber will repolarize, which closes the gates in the SR where Ca++ was being released. ATP-driven pumps will move Ca++ out of the sarcoplasm back into the SR. This results in the ''reshielding'' of the actin-binding sites on the thin filaments." (Choice A) II only is wrong because relaxation is a multi-step chain, not a single event, and tropomyosin re-covering the sites is the consequence of the preceding repolarization and \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) reuptake, not an isolated occurrence. (Choice B) I, then II, then III is wrong because it places repolarization and channel closure (III) last; in reality repolarization is the trigger that closes the SR release gates and must occur first, before \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) can be pumped back. (Choice C) II, then I, then III reverses the relationship between \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) reuptake and tropomyosin re-shielding: tropomyosin re-covers the sites only after sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) falls, so reuptake (I) must precede re-shielding (II), and repolarization (III) still cannot come last. (Choice D) III, then I, then II is correct because repolarization closes the SR channels, \(\text{ATP}\)-driven pumps then clear \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) from the sarcoplasm, and only then does tropomyosin re-shield the binding sites to end cross-bridge cycling. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the fixed causal sequence of skeletal muscle relaxation.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
