-- Q14 [Excitation-Contraction Coupling] medium skill4 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'A researcher records acetylcholine (ACh) levels in the synaptic cleft of a skeletal neuromuscular junction after a single motor-neuron action potential, with and without an acetylcholinesterase (AChE) inhibitor.

| Condition | ACh in cleft 50 ms after the impulse |
|---|---|
| No inhibitor | Undetectable |
| AChE inhibitor present | Remains elevated |

In the inhibitor condition, which outcome at the muscle fiber is most likely?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "End-plate hyperpolarization and muscle relaxation"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Prolonged end-plate depolarization and sustained contraction"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Halted acetylcholine release from the axon terminal"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A single brief twitch followed by rapid relaxation"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because ACh that lingers in the cleft keeps rebinding its receptor and reopening the cation channel, so the end-plate stays depolarized and the fiber keeps contracting. Normally AChE degrades ACh "so that the ACh cannot rebind to a receptor and reopen its channel, which would cause unwanted extended muscle excitation and contraction" (Anatomy & Physiology 2e, lines 17289-17291). When AChE is inhibited, "the acetylcholine stays in the cleft and can continually bind and unbind to postsynaptic receptors," increasing neurotransmission (Biology 2e, lines 46119-46121). Because ACh binding opens the receptor channel and lets positive ions in to depolarize the fiber (Anatomy & Physiology 2e, lines 17279-17281), persistent ACh produces a maintained end-plate depolarization that drives sustained contraction. (Choice A) End-plate hyperpolarization and relaxation reverses the direction of the response: ACh opens cation channels that depolarize the fiber, never hyperpolarize it (Anatomy & Physiology 2e, lines 17279-17281). (Choice B) is correct: Sustained receptor activation maintains the depolarized end-plate and the contraction, the very "extended muscle excitation and contraction" AChE normally prevents (Anatomy & Physiology 2e, lines 17289-17291). (Choice C) Halting ACh release confuses the postsynaptic clearance step with presynaptic release; AChE acts on ACh already in the cleft and does not control vesicular release from the axon terminal (Biology 2e, lines 51527-51529). (Choice D) A single brief twitch with rapid relaxation is what occurs with normal AChE clearance, not when ACh remains elevated and continually rebinds (Biology 2e, lines 46119-46121). This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the tabulated ACh levels to predict the downstream effect on end-plate depolarization and contraction.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
