-- Q15 [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] medium skill4 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'A researcher measures peak isometric tension generated by a skinned skeletal muscle fiber bathed in solutions of increasing free \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) concentration, with \(\text{ATP}\) held in excess.

| Sarcoplasmic \([\text{Ca}^{2+}]\) (\(\mu\text{M}\)) | Peak tension (% of max) |
|---|---|
| 0.05 | 3 |
| 0.5 | 22 |
| 1.0 | 58 |
| 3.0 | 91 |
| 10.0 | 99 |

Which mechanism most directly accounts for the rise in tension as \([\text{Ca}^{2+}]\) increases?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) binds troponin, shifting tropomyosin to expose more actin sites"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) binds calmodulin, activating myosin light-chain kinase"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) is pumped into the sarcoplasmic reticulum, reshielding actin sites"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) supplies the energy that re-cocks each detached myosin head"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because rising sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binds troponin, sliding tropomyosin off the actin filament so that progressively more myosin-binding sites are exposed for cross-bridge formation, and more cross-bridges generate more tension. In a resting fiber the troponin-tropomyosin complex covers the myosin-binding sites on actin and prevents the heads from attaching; the textbook states that "Troponin also has a binding site for \(\text{Ca}^{++}\) ions" and that "The first step in the process of contraction is for \(\text{Ca}^{++}\) to bind to troponin so that tropomyosin can slide away from the binding sites on the actin strands. This allows the myosin heads to bind to these exposed binding sites and form cross-bridges." Because the number of available actin sites scales with how much \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is bound to troponin, higher \([\text{Ca}^{2+}]\) recruits more cross-bridges and yields the higher tensions seen in the table; with \(\text{ATP}\) already in excess, \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) availability is the variable that controls site exposure. (Choice A) This is correct: \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) acts on troponin to displace tropomyosin and unshield actin sites, so tension tracks the fraction of sites exposed. (Choice B) Calmodulin and myosin light-chain kinase regulate contraction in smooth muscle, not skeletal muscle; the text notes "Because smooth muscle cells do not contain troponin, cross-bridge formation is not regulated by the troponin-tropomyosin complex but instead by the regulatory protein calmodulin," so this names the wrong regulatory protein for a skeletal fiber. (Choice C) Pumping \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) into the sarcoplasmic reticulum lowers sarcoplasmic \([\text{Ca}^{2+}]\) and "causes the tropomyosin to reshield... the binding sites," which drives relaxation, the reverse of the tension rise shown. (Choice D) \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) does not energize the cross-bridge cycle; re-cocking the myosin head "requires \(\text{ATP}\)," and the prompt already holds \(\text{ATP}\) in excess, so this confuses the energy source with the regulatory trigger. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the trend in the table and reason from the tension-versus-\([\text{Ca}^{2+}]\) relationship to the underlying molecular mechanism.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 110)
  RETURNING id
