-- Q16 [Excitation-Contraction Coupling] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'A researcher records from a single skeletal muscle fiber and finds that once a stimulus depolarizes the fiber to threshold, every suprathreshold stimulus produces a twitch of identical peak tension. To generate progressively greater force from the whole muscle the fiber belongs to, the nervous system most directly relies on which mechanism?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Driving each fiber to contract more partially"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Recruiting additional motor units in the muscle"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Generating a larger action potential per fiber"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Lengthening the relaxation phase of each twitch"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because a single fiber responds all-or-none, so whole-muscle force is graded by activating more motor units rather than by changing how hard any one fiber contracts. The textbook states that "this increasing activation of motor units produces an increase in muscle contraction known as recruitment. As more motor units are recruited, the muscle contraction grows progressively stronger." Because a fiber that reaches threshold fires an identical, all-or-none action potential, "a stronger stimulus, which might depolarize the membrane well past threshold, will not make a ''bigger'' action potential," and you will not "have a stronger muscle contraction, because of the size of the action potential because they are not different sizes." The graded muscle response therefore depends on "the number of motor neurons transmitting action potentials," meaning the nervous system enlists additional motor units to raise force. (Choice A) A fiber at threshold contracts fully, not partially; the all-or-none property means there is no graded per-fiber contraction to dial up, so partial contraction is not the mechanism. (Choice B) Recruitment is correct because force is added in steps by activating progressively larger, higher-threshold motor units, allowing the same muscle to lift a feather or a heavy weight. (Choice C) Action potentials are all-or-none, so "one action potential is not bigger than another"; a stronger stimulus cannot enlarge the action potential or the resulting twitch of a single fiber. (Choice D) Prolonging relaxation would describe twitch timing, not added force; greater sustained tension instead arises from wave summation at high firing frequency or from recruiting more motor units, not from lengthening relaxation. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that a single fiber obeys the all-or-none law and that graded whole-muscle force is produced by motor unit recruitment.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
