  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'A myogram records a skeletal muscle twitch, and tension remains flat at baseline during the brief interval immediately following the motor neuron''s stimulus before any tension rise appears. During this flat interval, the events occurring in the muscle fiber are most directly described as which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Cross-bridges cycling as sarcomeres actively shorten"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) being pumped from the sarcoplasm back into the SR"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tropomyosin shifting off actin as cross-bridges form"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The action potential propagating while \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) is released from the SR"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because the flat, pre-tension interval of a twitch is the latent period, during which the action potential is being propagated along the sarcolemma and \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) ions are released from the SR while contraction has yet to occur. The textbook states that the first phase is the latent period, "during which the action potential is being propagated along the sarcolemma and Ca++ ions are released from the SR" and that "excitation and contraction are being coupled but contraction has yet to occur." Tension stays at baseline precisely because cross-bridge cycling and sarcomere shortening have not yet begun; those events define the subsequent contraction phase, when tension rises. (Choice A) Cross-bridges cycling as sarcomeres actively shorten occurs in the contraction phase, where "cross-bridges have formed, and sarcomeres are actively shortening to the point of peak tension"; this produces the tension rise, not the flat latent interval. (Choice B) Pumping \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) from the sarcoplasm back into the SR belongs to the relaxation phase, when "Ca++ ions are pumped out of the sarcoplasm into the SR, and cross-bridge cycling stops," which is the opposite direction of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) movement from the latent period''s release. (Choice C) Tropomyosin shifting off actin as cross-bridges form is part of the contraction phase, where "tropomyosin has shifted away from myosin-binding sites on actin, cross-bridges have formed"; although \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release has occurred by the latent period, the filament-level steps of tropomyosin movement and cross-bridge formation have not yet happened. (Choice D) Correctly captures the latent period because excitation-contraction coupling—AP propagation plus SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release—is underway while no shortening has begun, keeping tension at baseline. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall which molecular events define the latent period of a twitch versus the contraction and relaxation phases.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
