  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'Several hours after death, a skeletal muscle fiber has exhausted its \(\text{ATP}\) supply while myosin heads are still bound to actin in formed cross-bridges. Which outcome most directly explains the rigidity that develops in the muscle?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Myosin heads stay locked onto actin and cannot detach"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Cross-bridges cycle rapidly, producing repeated twitches"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Tropomyosin re-covers the actin binding sites, relaxing the fiber"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Myosin heads detach but cannot return to the cocked position"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because the binding of \(\text{ATP}\) to the myosin head is the step that causes it to release from actin, so without any \(\text{ATP}\) the heads remain locked onto the actin-binding sites. According to the textbook, \"ATP binding causes the myosin head to detach from the actin,\" and conversely \"In the absence of ATP, the myosin head will not detach from actin.\" When a person dies, ATP production stops, and \"there is no ATP available for myosin heads to detach from the actin-binding sites, so the cross-bridges stay in place, causing the rigidity in the skeletal muscles\" — the state known as rigor mortis. Because cross-bridges were already formed at the moment ATP ran out, they freeze in the bound configuration rather than completing the cycle. (Choice A) is correct because detachment of myosin from actin specifically requires a new \(\text{ATP}\) molecule to bind the head; with the supply gone, every engaged cross-bridge is held fast, producing the characteristic stiffness. (Choice B) is wrong because rapid cross-bridge cycling and twitching depend on continuous \(\text{ATP}\) to detach, re-cock, and reattach the heads, which is exactly what is missing; this reverses the actual relationship between ATP and cycling. (Choice C) is wrong because tropomyosin re-covers the binding sites only after \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is actively pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum, an ATP-requiring process that cannot occur here and that would produce relaxation, not rigidity. (Choice D) is wrong because it misidentifies the blocked step: without \(\text{ATP}\) the heads cannot detach in the first place, so they never reach a re-cocking stage. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that ATP binding triggers myosin detachment to predict the mechanical outcome when ATP is absent.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
