WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'A physiologist applies a drug to an isolated skeletal muscle fiber and records the following before and after exposure:

| Event measured | Before drug | After drug |
|---|---|---|
| Sarcolemmal action potential | Normal | Normal |
| T-tubule depolarization | Normal | Normal |
| \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum | Normal | Blocked |

The fiber no longer generates tension. The contraction step that most directly fails is the one in which:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The action potential propagates along the sarcolemma"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Myosin heads detach from actin as \\(\\text{ATP}\\) binds"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Calmodulin activates cross-bridge formation on actin"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Tropomyosin slides off the myosin-binding sites on actin"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because blocking \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR) starves troponin of the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) it needs to shift tropomyosin off the actin''s myosin-binding sites. The action potential along the sarcolemma and into the T-tubules is recorded as normal, so excitation is intact; the lesion lies at the step where the T-tubule signal would normally trigger SR channels to release \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) into the sarcoplasm (textbook: \"The T-tubules carry the action potential into the interior of the cell, which triggers the opening of calcium channels in the membrane of the adjacent SR, causing Ca++ to diffuse out of the SR and into the sarcoplasm\"). Without sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\), the first molecular step of contraction cannot occur: \"The first step in the process of contraction is for Ca++ to bind to troponin so that tropomyosin can slide away from the binding sites on the actin strands.\" Because tropomyosin stays in place over the myosin-binding sites, no cross-bridges form and no tension is generated. (Choice A) The action potential propagating along the sarcolemma is recorded as Normal both before and after the drug, so this upstream step is intact, not the point of failure. (Choice B) \(\text{ATP}\)-dependent detachment of myosin heads is a step of the cross-bridge cycle that occurs only after cross-bridges have already formed; here cross-bridges never form, and the drug blocks \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release, not \(\text{ATP}\) supply, so detachment is not the failed step. (Choice C) Calmodulin is the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-binding regulatory protein of smooth muscle, which lacks troponin; in skeletal muscle the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) sensor that uncovers the binding sites is troponin, not calmodulin. (Choice D) With SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release blocked, troponin cannot bind \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\), so tropomyosin never slides off the myosin-binding sites, correctly localizing the failure to the uncovering step. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the table showing a normal sarcolemmal action potential alongside blocked SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release and reason to the specific molecular contraction step that fails.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
