-- Q20 [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] hard skill4 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'A researcher applies a compound to skinned skeletal muscle fibers and assays each step of the cross-bridge cycle for individual myosin heads:

| Step assayed | Result |
|---|---|
| Head binds an exposed actin site | Occurs normally |
| \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release and power stroke (pivot toward M-line) | Occurs normally |
| \(\text{ADP}\) release after the power stroke | Occurs normally |
| \(\text{ATP}\) binding and detachment from actin | Occurs normally |
| Return of the head to the cocked, high-energy position | Does NOT occur |

The compound most directly disrupts which event?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Release of \\(\\text{P}_\\text{i}\\) from the bound myosin head"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Binding of \\(\\text{ATP}\\) that detaches myosin from actin"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Release of \\(\\text{ADP}\\) at the end of the power stroke"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Hydrolysis of \\(\\text{ATP}\\) by the myosin head''s intrinsic ATPase"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because the head returns to its cocked, high-energy position only when the myosin''s intrinsic ATPase hydrolyzes the bound \(\text{ATP}\) to \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\), so a block confined to re-cocking must lie at that hydrolysis step. After \(\text{ATP}\) binding detaches the head from actin, "ATP is converted to ADP and Pi by the intrinsic ATPase activity of myosin," and "the energy released during ATP hydrolysis changes the angle of the myosin head into a cocked position." Because the table shows actin binding, \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release with a normal power stroke, \(\text{ADP}\) release, and \(\text{ATP}\)-driven detachment all proceeding normally, every event upstream of re-cocking is intact; the only lesion sits at the conversion of the detached head''s bound \(\text{ATP}\) into \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\), which is what supplies the energy to re-cock the head for the next cycle. (Choice A) \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release precedes and drives the power stroke—"Pi is then released, causing myosin to form a stronger attachment to the actin, after which the myosin head moves toward the M-line"—so if \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release were blocked the power stroke would fail, yet the table shows the power stroke occurring normally. (Choice B) \(\text{ATP}\) binding is the event that detaches the head ("ATP binding causes the myosin head to detach from the actin"); detachment is recorded as normal, so this step is intact. (Choice C) \(\text{ADP}\) release happens at the end of the power stroke ("After the power stroke, ADP is released"), a step the table also marks as normal, and it occurs before \(\text{ATP}\) ever binds, so it cannot account for a failure to re-cock. (Choice D) Re-cocking is powered specifically by myosin''s intrinsic ATPase hydrolyzing \(\text{ATP}\), the one step the table flags as absent, making it the precise lesion. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the per-step results in the table to localize the defect to the single mechanistic step that produces the cocked position.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
