-- Q21 [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] hard skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'A researcher applies a drug that selectively inactivates the \(\text{ATP}\)-driven \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) pump in the membrane of the sarcoplasmic reticulum of a skeletal muscle fiber, while the motor neuron stops firing and acetylcholinesterase remains fully active. Which outcome is the most likely consequence for this fiber after the action potential ends?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The fiber relaxes more rapidly than an untreated fiber"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The fiber stays contracted because acetylcholine keeps binding its receptor"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The fiber stays contracted because sarcoplasmic \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) cannot fall and tropomyosin cannot reshield the actin sites"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The fiber relaxes normally as soon as it depletes its \\(\\text{ATP}\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because relaxation requires the SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) pump to clear \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) from the sarcoplasm, and with that pump disabled the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) stays high, troponin stays bound, and tropomyosin never re-covers the actin-binding sites. The textbook states that during relaxation, "ATP-driven pumps will move Ca++ out of the sarcoplasm back into the SR. This results in the ''reshielding'' of the actin-binding sites on the thin filaments" (lines 17539-17540), so cross-bridge cycling can continue only while \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) remains elevated. Because the drug stops \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) from being pumped back, the sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) cannot fall, tropomyosin cannot reshield, and the fiber cannot lose its tension. The lesion is downstream of the neuromuscular junction, so a normally functioning acetylcholinesterase cannot rescue relaxation. (Choice A) Faster relaxation reverses the predicted direction; with the pump gone, \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) removal is the rate-limiting step for relaxation, so relaxation is blocked rather than accelerated. (Choice B) Sustained acetylcholine binding describes an acetylcholinesterase failure at the synapse, where ACh "cannot rebind to a receptor and reopen its channel, which would cause unwanted extended muscle excitation" (lines 17290-17291); here AChE is intact and the neuron has stopped firing, so the synapse is not the problem. (Choice C) is correct: With the SR pump inactivated, \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) remains in the sarcoplasm bound to troponin, tropomyosin stays shifted off the binding sites, and the fiber is held in a contracted state. (Choice D) ATP depletion does stop contraction by ending cross-bridge cycling, but that is fatigue/rigor under a different condition, not the normal pump-driven relaxation, and the question specifies pump failure rather than ATP loss. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relaxation mechanism to predict the effect of a specific molecular lesion and rule out an upstream synaptic cause.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
