-- Q22 [Excitation-Contraction Coupling] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'A toxin binds the nicotinic acetylcholine receptors of the motor end-plate and prevents their ion channels from opening, while the motor neuron continues to fire action potentials and release \(\text{ACh}\) into the synaptic cleft normally. Contraction of the muscle fiber is most directly abolished because the toxin prevents which event?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Depolarization of the motor end-plate sarcolemma"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Release of \\(\\text{ACh}\\) from the axon terminal"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Diffusion of \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) out of the sarcoplasmic reticulum"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Reuptake of \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) into the sarcoplasmic reticulum"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because blocking the nicotinic receptor channel stops the positive ions that would otherwise enter and depolarize the end-plate, so no muscle action potential and no contraction can follow. When \(\text{ACh}\) binds its receptor at the motor end-plate, "a channel in the ACh receptor opens and positively charged ions can pass through into the muscle fiber, causing it to depolarize." This end-plate depolarization is what triggers the voltage-gated sodium channels and the muscle action potential that "spreads ... along the entire membrane to initiate excitation-contraction coupling." With the receptor channel held shut, normal upstream \(\text{ACh}\) release is irrelevant because the postsynaptic membrane never depolarizes, so the action potential, the T-tubule signal, and SR \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) release never occur. (Choice A) Correct: the receptor is the gate for the depolarizing inward current, and blocking it severs excitation right at the end-plate, upstream of the T-tubule and SR. (Choice B) \(\text{ACh}\) release is a presynaptic event driven by the axon-terminal action potential and voltage-gated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) entry into the end bulb; the stem states release is normal, and a postsynaptic receptor block cannot affect it. (Choice C) \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) diffusing out of the SR is blocked here too, but only as a downstream consequence of the missing end-plate depolarization, making it less direct than the receptor-level event that the toxin actually targets. (Choice D) Reuptake of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) into the SR is the relaxation step that ends contraction; it is the opposite stage of the cycle from the excitation event the toxin abolishes. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the neuromuscular signaling sequence to predict where a receptor-channel block interrupts excitation-contraction coupling.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
