-- Q23 [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'A researcher reconstitutes skeletal muscle myosin with actin filaments and pulses the system with calcium so that the actin-binding sites are exposed. At the precise instant a myosin head first latches onto an actin-binding site to begin a new cross-bridge, which nucleotide state is most likely occupying the myosin head?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{ATP}\\) freshly bound, before hydrolysis"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{ADP}\\) and \\(\\text{P}_\\text{i}\\) both still bound"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{ADP}\\) alone, with \\(\\text{P}_\\text{i}\\) already released"}, {"label": "D", "text": "No nucleotide bound, in the rigor state"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because a myosin head first attaches to actin while it is still carrying the products of \(\text{ATP}\) hydrolysis, \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\). In the cross-bridge cycle, \(\text{ATP}\) binding detaches the head; the intrinsic ATPase of myosin then hydrolyzes that \(\text{ATP}\) to \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\), cocking the head into a high-energy configuration that still holds both products. The textbook states that ''Cross-bridge formation occurs when the myosin head attaches to the actin while adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and inorganic phosphate (Pi) are still bound to myosin,'' and only after attachment is ''Pi then released, causing myosin to form a stronger attachment to the actin,'' with \(\text{ADP}\) released still later at the end of the power stroke. (Choice A) A freshly bound \(\text{ATP}\) is wrong because ''ATP binding causes the myosin head to detach from the actin''; the head must hydrolyze that \(\text{ATP}\) to its products before it can re-attach, so \(\text{ATP}\)-bound is the detached state, not the attaching one. (Choice B) is correct: at the moment of initial latch-on the cocked head still holds both \(\text{ADP}\) and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\), and \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) release follows to strengthen the bond and trigger the power stroke. (Choice C) \(\text{ADP}\) alone describes the strong-bound state that exists only after \(\text{P}_\text{i}\) has been released during the power stroke, since ''Pi is then released'' occurs after the head is already bound, so it is one timing step past the initial attachment. (Choice D) The nucleotide-free rigor state arises only when no \(\text{ATP}\) is available, so the cross-bridges ''stay in place'' and cannot detach (as in rigor mortis); it is a locked end state, not the configuration that forms a fresh cross-bridge. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the precise nucleotide occupancy of the myosin head at each step of the cross-bridge cycle.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
