-- Q24 [Excitation-Contraction Coupling] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Excitation-Contraction Coupling', 'A motor neuron''s action potential reaches the axon terminal at a neuromuscular junction, but an experimental toxin selectively blocks the voltage-gated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels in the membrane of the synaptic end bulb while leaving the muscle fiber''s internal \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) stores intact. Which event is most directly prevented by this block?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Binding of \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) to troponin on the thin filament"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Fusion of acetylcholine-containing vesicles with the presynaptic membrane"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Reuptake of \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) into the sarcoplasmic reticulum"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Diffusion of acetylcholine across the synaptic cleft"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) that enters the synaptic end bulb is what facilitates the merging of neurotransmitter vesicles with the presynaptic membrane, so blocking that channel directly prevents acetylcholine vesicle fusion. When an action potential reaches the axon terminals, voltage-gated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels in the synaptic end bulb open, the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) concentration inside the bulb rises, and the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) associates with proteins on the outer surface of neurotransmitter vesicles to drive their fusion and exocytosis of acetylcholine into the synaptic cleft (lines 22225-22229). This presynaptic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) pool is entirely distinct from the sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) released later from the SR inside the fiber, which the stem specifies remains intact. (Choice A) Binding of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) to troponin uses the separate sarcoplasmic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) released from the SR within the fiber, not the presynaptic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) entering the end bulb, so it is not what this toxin blocks. (Choice B) is correct because presynaptic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) entry is the immediate trigger for vesicle fusion, the one step that depends on the channels being blocked; downstream events fail only because no acetylcholine is released. (Choice C) Reuptake of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) into the SR is a relaxation step driven by SR pumps inside the fiber and does not depend on presynaptic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels. (Choice D) Diffusion of acetylcholine across the cleft is downstream of vesicle fusion and would fail only as a consequence of blocked fusion; it is the fusion step, not diffusion, that the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) block prevents most directly. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between two separate \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) pools to predict which step a targeted channel block disrupts.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
