-- Q25 [Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament] hard skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cross-Bridge Cycle & Sliding Filament', 'A skeletal muscle fiber is experimentally depleted of \(\text{ATP}\) while its sarcoplasm still contains \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) bound to troponin. The actin-binding sites remain exposed and cross-bridges stay locked in place. The persistence of exposed actin sites in this fiber is most directly determined by which factor?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Myosin heads remain attached because \\(\\text{ATP}\\) is absent"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) pumps fail to return \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) to the sarcoplasmic reticulum"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\)-bound troponin holds tropomyosin off the binding sites"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{ATP}\\) hydrolysis is needed to re-cock the myosin heads"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because whether actin-binding sites are exposed is set solely by the position of tropomyosin, which is held away from those sites as long as \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-bound troponin keeps it shifted. The textbook states that "for \(\text{Ca}^{++}\) to bind to troponin so that tropomyosin can slide away from the binding sites on the actin strands... allows the myosin heads to bind to these exposed binding sites," and that reshielding occurs only when "\(\text{Ca}^{++}\) ions are then pumped back into the SR, which causes the tropomyosin to reshield... the binding sites." Because \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is still bound to troponin here, tropomyosin stays off the sites and the sites stay exposed regardless of the \(\text{ATP}\) level. The separate fact that the cross-bridges cannot release is governed by \(\text{ATP}\): "In the absence of \(\text{ATP}\), the myosin head will not detach from actin," producing rigor — but that controls bridge detachment, not the exposure of the actin sites themselves. (Choice A) Absence of \(\text{ATP}\) does explain why the formed cross-bridges stay locked, but it does not determine whether the underlying actin sites are exposed; site exposure is a tropomyosin-position question, not a myosin-detachment question. (Choice B) Pump failure keeping \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) in the sarcoplasm is the upstream reason \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is still on troponin, but the immediate gating variable for site exposure is tropomyosin''s position, the step the persisting \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-troponin binding acts through. (Choice C) Correct: tropomyosin position is the direct gating variable for actin-site exposure, and \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-bound troponin keeps tropomyosin displaced, so as long as \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) remains on troponin the sites stay uncovered. (Choice D) Re-cocking the myosin head via \(\text{ATP}\) hydrolysis is part of the cross-bridge cycle and is blocked when \(\text{ATP}\) is absent, but re-cocking is irrelevant to whether tropomyosin uncovers the actin sites. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that tropomyosin position, controlled by \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-bound troponin, is the variable that gates actin-site exposure, distinct from the \(\text{ATP}\)-dependent detachment that fails in rigor.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
